--TEST--
`Core\Response::json` throws on a value JSON cannot spell and leaves the body untouched, because a refusal that has already written half a document is not a refusal -- `rule:security/response-body-is-one-typed-member`, spec § 10
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The boundary this member stops at is `Core\Json::encode`'s own, reached
// through the same encoder: a non-finite `float` and a `bytes` buffer are two
// of the three values that `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R4 makes throw rather than answer falsy.
try {
    Core\Response::json(Core\Math::sqrt(-1.0));
    echo "not refused\n";
} catch (LogicError $nan) {
    echo $nan->message, "\n";
}

// Nothing was written on the way to that throw. Asserted through a capture,
// which is `rule:security/capture-answers-the-carrier`'s redirection of the same output a body member writes
// to -- a member that serialized into the body and then discovered the value
// was unencodable would show up here as a partial document.
var $written = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    try {
        Core\Response::json(["ok" => 1, "bad" => "text" as bytes]);
    } catch (LogicError $binary) {
    }
});
echo "wrote [", $written, "]\n";

// The declaration is not made either -- it is recorded only once the value has
// serialized -- so a request that caught this and answered some other way is
// not carrying `application/json` over a body that is not JSON. That half is
// asserted in `nvs-server`, where a `Content-Type` is observable at all.
Core\Response::json(["ok" => 1]);
echo "\n";
--EXPECT--
Core\Response::json(): `NAN` has no JSON spelling — JSON has no `NaN` and no `Infinity`
wrote []
{"ok":1}
